  '{"en":"<p class=\"lead\">If you catch yourself putting reading glasses on to check your phone, then taking them off to glance across the room, then putting them back on to read a menu — you are not alone. The constant switch between near and far is one of the most common frustrations for adults in their fifties. Progressive lenses promise a single-pair solution. But are they the right answer for you, or would a dedicated pair of reading glasses do the job better?</p>\n\n<p>This guide breaks down the real differences between progressive lenses and reading glasses — not the marketing version, the practical one — so you can make a confident decision.</p>\n\n<h2>What progressive lenses actually do</h2>\n<p>Progressive lenses (also called no-line multifocals) combine three viewing zones in one seamless lens:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Distance zone</strong> (top) — driving, walking, screens across the room</li>\n<li><strong>Intermediate zone</strong> (middle) — computer, dashboard, kitchen counter</li>\n<li><strong>Near zone</strong> (bottom) — phone, book, menu, close-up detail</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Unlike traditional bifocals, there is no visible line splitting the lens. The transition between zones is gradual and invisible to anyone looking at you. You get one pair of glasses that handles everything from across the room to arm\u2019s length.</p>\n<p>The trade-off: your eyes need time to learn where each zone sits. Most people adapt within one to two weeks. A small number need up to three weeks, which is why lool backs every progressive order with a <strong>3-week adaptation guarantee</strong>.</p>\n\n<h2>What reading glasses do</h2>\n<p>Reading glasses have a single magnification strength across the entire lens — typically +1.0 to +3.5. They do one thing well: make close-up text sharp and comfortable.</p>\n<p>They are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Simple</strong> — no adaptation period, put them on and read</li>\n<li><strong>Affordable</strong> — lower price than progressive lenses</li>\n<li><strong>Instant</strong> — no waiting, no learning curve</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The limitation: reading glasses only work at close range. Anything beyond arm\u2019s length becomes blurry. If you need to look up at a whiteboard, check a rearview mirror, or scan a grocery shelf, you have to remove them or peer over the top.</p>\n\n<h2>The head-to-head comparison</h2>\n<table style=\"width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:1.5rem 0\">\n<thead>\n<tr style=\"border-bottom:2px solid #e5e5e5;text-align:left\">\n<th style=\"padding:0.75rem 0.5rem\">Factor</th>\n<th style=\"padding:0.75rem 0.5rem\">Progressive lenses</th>\n<th style=\"padding:0.75rem 0.5rem\">Reading glasses</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #e5e5e5\">\n<td style=\"padding:0.75rem 0.5rem;font-weight:600\">Viewing range</td>\n<td style=\"padding:0.75rem 0.5rem\">Distance + intermediate + near (all-in-one)</td>\n<td style=\"padding:0.75rem 0.5rem\">Near only</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #e5e5e5\">\n<td style=\"padding:0.75rem 0.5rem;font-weight:600\">Adaptation</td>\n<td style=\"padding:0.75rem 0.5rem\">1\u20133 weeks (guaranteed at lool)</td>\n<td style=\"padding:0.75rem 0.5rem\">None</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #e5e5e5\">\n<td style=\"padding:0.75rem 0.5rem;font-weight:600\">Price</td>\n<td style=\"padding:0.75rem 0.5rem\">Higher (+ $89 at lool)</td>\n<td style=\"padding:0.75rem 0.5rem\">Lower</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #e5e5e5\">\n<td style=\"padding:0.75rem 0.5rem;font-weight:600\">Switching between pairs</td>\n<td style=\"padding:0.75rem 0.5rem\">One pair does it all</td>\n<td style=\"padding:0.75rem 0.5rem\">May need separate distance pair</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #e5e5e5\">\n<td style=\"padding:0.75rem 0.5rem;font-weight:600\">Best for</td>\n<td style=\"padding:0.75rem 0.5rem\">Multi-distance daily life</td>\n<td style=\"padding:0.75rem 0.5rem\">Dedicated close-up tasks</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h2>Who progressive lenses are for</h2>\n<p>Progressive lenses are a lifestyle decision, not a prescription decision. The best candidates are people who:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Already wear readers most days and find themselves putting them on and removing them constantly throughout the day</li>\n<li>Need clear vision at multiple distances daily — computer work, reading, cooking, driving</li>\n<li>Want one pair instead of juggling two or three</li>\n<li>Are willing to commit to wearing the progressives exclusively during the adaptation period</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Reading glasses alone are sufficient if you:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Only need near correction and your distance vision is still sharp</li>\n<li>Work primarily at a computer (a dedicated intermediate pair may be more comfortable)</li>\n<li>Prezero the idea of an adjustment period</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>The 3-week adaptation timeline: what to expect</h2>\n<p>Progressive lenses work by placing different focal powers in different zones of the same lens. Your brain needs to learn which zone to look through for each task. This is a learnable skill — not a permanent discomfort.</p>\n<p><strong>Days 1–3:</strong> You may feel slight swaying when walking or a narrow \"sweet spot\" for reading. This is normal. Your peripheral vision is detecting the transition zones.</p>\n<p><strong>Days 4–7:</strong> Your brain starts routing the correct zone automatically. Reading becomes comfortable. Walking downstairs improves noticeably.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 2:</strong> Most people are fully adapted by now. You stop thinking about the lens zones.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 3:</strong> If adaptation has not happened by day 21, lool replaces the lenses with single-vision readers at no extra cost. No questions, no paperwork.</p>\n\n<h2>5 practical tips to adapt faster</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Start in the morning.</strong> Put your progressives on first thing when your eyes are fresh. Do not alternate between old and new glasses during the adaptation period.</li>\n<li><strong>Begin with simple tasks.</strong> Reading a book and walking in familiar places are easier than complex visual environments like busy streets or scrolling feeds.</li>\n<li><strong>Point your nose at what you want to see.</strong> Turn your head toward the object rather than shifting only your eyes. This trains your brain to find the correct lens zone faster.</li>\n<li><strong>Avoid quick head movements at first.</strong> Smooth, deliberate movements give your visual system time to adjust.</li>\n<li><strong>Commit to at least five full days.</strong> Wear the progressive pair all waking hours. Resist the urge to switch back to your old readers — the first few days of mild discomfort are the investment that pays off by week two.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h2>How to choose the right frame for progressives</h2>\n<p>Frame height matters more with progressive lenses than with single-vision readers. Taller lenses provide a wider intermediate zone, making the transition between distances smoother.</p>\n<p>Two lool frames that work well with progressives:</p>\n<h3><a href=\"/en/product/lool-tr90-square\">TR90 Square Fashion Readers</a></h3>\n<p>At 19.1 g, this oversized square frame gives progressive lenses enough vertical space for comfortable zone transitions. The TR90 material is flexible and durable — ideal if you are wearing glasses more hours per day than you are used to.</p>\n<h3><a href=\"/en/product/lool-8663\">Boston Oval Fashion Readers</a></h3>\n<p>The classic round-oval shape offers a slightly more traditional look with enough lens height for progressive corridor placement. A strong choice if you prefer a warm, intellectual aesthetic.</p>\n<p>Before you order, use the <a href=\"/en/try-on\">virtual try-on</a> to see how each frame sits on your face with your real camera — frame fit directly affects progressive lens performance.</p>\n\n<h2>The PD measurement that matters</h2>\n<p>Accurate pupillary distance (PD) is critical for progressive lenses. The progressive corridor is narrow, and even a 1–2 mm offset can make adaptation harder.</p>\n<p>lool provides a <a href=\"/en/measure-pd\">home PD measurement tool</a> that uses your phone camera to capture an accurate reading. Take the measurement twice and use the average — consistency between the two readings is your confidence check.</p>\n\n<h2>Things to know before you order</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Progressive lenses add $89</strong> to the frame price at lool. This covers the adaptation guarantee and premium lens materials.</li>\n<li><strong>All progressive lenses at lool</strong> include blue-light filtering, UV400 protection, and scratch-resistant coating as standard.</li>\n<li><strong>Free tracked shipping</strong> on orders over $69. <strong>30-day returns</strong> on non-prescription glasses.</li>\n<li><strong>Prescription required.</strong> Progressive lenses need a current prescription with an \"add\" value for near correction. If your last eye exam is more than 12 months old, get an updated one before ordering.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Do progressives work for computer use?</h2>\n<p>Most progressive designs include an intermediate zone optimized for screen distance (about 60–80 cm). However, the intermediate corridor is narrower than what a dedicated computer pair would offer.</p>\n<p>If you spend 6+ hours daily at a computer, a separate intermediate pair may be more comfortable for long screen sessions. If your day mixes tasks at various distances, progressives handle the variety well.</p>\n\n<h2>The bottom line</h2>\n<p>The question is not whether progressive lenses are better than reading glasses. It is whether the problem you are trying to solve is <em>near vision</em> or <em>constant switching</em>.</p>\n<p>If near vision is the only issue, reading glasses are the simpler and more affordable answer. If the constant switching is the real frustration — putting glasses on and off multiple times an hour, every day — progressive lenses solve it with a single pair and a short adaptation period backed by a 3-week guarantee.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/en/products\">Browse our frame collection →</a></p>\n\n<hr/>\n\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>Can everyone adapt to progressive lenses?</h3>\n<p>The vast majority of people adapt within one to two weeks of consistent daily wear. A small percentage may need the full three weeks. At lool, if adaptation has not happened by day 21, we replace the lenses with single-vision readers at no cost — so there is no financial risk in trying.</p>\n<h3>How long does adaptation actually take?</h3>\n<p>Most wearers report feeling comfortable between day 4 and day 7. Full adaptation — where you stop thinking about the lens zones — usually happens by the end of week 2. Three weeks is the outer boundary, and our guarantee covers that range.</p>\n<h3>What if I cannot adapt?</h3>\n<p>With lool\u2019s 3-week guarantee, you simply contact us and we replace the lenses with single-vision readers at no charge. No return shipping fees, no restocking fees, no questions.</p>\n<h3>Are progressive lenses more expensive than reading glasses?</h3>\n<p>At lool, progressive lenses add $89 to the frame price. This includes premium multifocal lenses, blue-light filtering, UV400, scratch-resistant coating, and the 3-week adaptation guarantee. Non-prescription reading glasses are priced at the frame-only level.</p>\n<h3>Do I need a prescription for progressive lenses?</h3>\n<p>Yes. Progressive lenses require a current prescription that includes an \"add\" value — the additional magnification for near vision. If your last eye exam was more than 12 months ago, update it before ordering.</p>\n<h3>Can I use my existing frames for progressive lenses?</h3>\n<p>Progressive lenses require frames with sufficient lens height for the three zones. Not all frames qualify. Browse our <a href=\"/en/products\">frame collection</a> for styles verified to work with progressives.</p>\n<h3>What is the return policy?</h3>\n<p>lool offers free tracked shipping on orders over $69 and 30-day free returns on non-prescription glasses. For progressive (prescription) orders, the 3-week adaptation guarantee applies instead of the standard return window.</p>"}',
